//! The WebKit backend (macOS): each view is a `WKWebView`, a native child view of the
//! platform-window window it opens in, drawn over the Silk UI at its hosted node and clipped to
//! the node's visible part. The Objective-C side is native/macos/bridge.m; the Silk side is
//! `bridge.BridgeWebViews`, shared with the WebView2 backend.
//!
//! Start platform-window's `MacPlatform` and open the window first; views live in its windows
//! and run on its main thread. Every event the bridge queues wakes the window's `nextEvent`
//! (which may then return `None`), so drain `takeEvents` after each turn of the loop.
//!
//! Agent commands evaluate agent.silk's calls in a script world of their own, which pages
//! cannot see, then click and type with synthesized `NSEvent`s, so pages get trusted input.
//! `OpenDevTools` is `Unsupported` (WebKit has no API to open Web Inspector; with
//! `inspectable` on, Safari's Develop menu attaches to the view).
import bridge { BridgeWebViews }
pub import bridge { ViewFrame }

pub type MacWebViews = BridgeWebViews
